    "media:imagetext": "#8\nNanking, November 8, 1937.\nDear Family:\nToday was bright and clear - not a cloud in the sky.\nJaps bombed a passenger train north of here killing two hundred innocent\nnon-combattants,\nbut they didn't bomb Nanking, for which no doubt we have\nthe Brussels conference\nto thank.\nMr. Peck\nand I had dinner with some\nold friends, Mr. and Mrs.Pan of the Ministry of Railways.\nesting to hear\nfirst hand of the marvelous job the railroads are doing -\nthey keep the trains moving under almost impossible odds.\nIn one instance\nover 1000 bombs of various sizes were dropped on one short stretch of\ntrack but within twelve hours trains were again operating over that stretch\nof roadbed.\nThe repair crews work at night and sleep in dugouts during\nthe day time.\nAt Soochow they had an average of eight hours of bombing\nfor ten conescutive days, but\nstill kept the trains moving.\nIf that sort\nof morale can pervade all services,\nthe Japs are going to have a long and\ntough job ahead.\nNovember 9th.\nBeautiful day - no bomb raids. Chinese are asking bide\non 60 basic trainer airplanes - they must think the war will last a long\ntime. The Board meeting was postponed until tomorrow - d--- it! I had\nplanned to leave tomorrow.\nBond can't get back to Nanking by car because\nall roads are blocked.\nHe wired that he was coming by British boat and\ndriving by car around the boom in the Langtsze.\nHe expects to arrive\nhere on Friday or Saturday.\nWill probably wait until he comes as the\nboat I was planning to take icaves tomorrow morning and there probably\nwill not be another until the one he comes on.\nSomeone - probably a paint salesman - sold the Chinese on the\nidea of painting all their bullaings a dirty gray color - supposedly so\nthey will not be so conspicuous from the air.\nOf course, all the bombing\nis done from great heights and about the only thing the bomber can see is\nroofs - but nearly every building is covered with that awful looking stuff -\neven statues, Ming Tomb Memorial and the Ningxim Sun Yat Sen Memorial.\nDon't know why the Japs overlooked that Memorial and the Ming Tombs, but\nthough outside the wall, neither of these \"military objectives\" have been\nbombed.\nI predict they will be, and when a howl is raised, the Japs will\nsay that anti-aircraft guns were there.\nOf course, the Chinese have been\nvery careful not to give the Japanese any valla reason for bombing the tomb\nof their George Washington.\nDined tonight with some of the American teachers at Nanking\nUniversity and some missionaries.\nIt was easy to tell which were which -\nmost of the teachers took a cocktail and smoked.\nThe missionaries may\ndo both - like a debutante raiüing the ice-box when she gets home - but\nwith the Ambassador and Mr. Peck both present, they were on their best\nbehavior and wore the proper funereal gravity which 1s the mode among\nmissionaries when there is laughter or anyone is having a good time.\nWednesday, November 10th.\nAs I write this I am waiting for a meeting\nof the Board of Directors of CNAC - the meeting was called for 3 o'clock\nbut on arriving punctually I am told in true,\nvery polite Chinese fashion\nthat the meeting will not be held until 4. Of course, they did not think\nto call me up. At noon we had an air raid - the first alarm came at 12:20 -\nN 8E01\nPIOS",
